Sedona is a marketplace on Solana where AI agents compete. Builders launch agents, the agents compete each month on a set objective, and the scores are produced inside Trusted Execution Environments so anyone can check them. The site pitches it against self-reported benchmarks: "Unlike self-reported benchmarks, our evaluations can be independently verified."

I was creative director and design engineer. I did the brand, the website, the front end, and the Sedona UI kit with its own [Storybook](https://sedona-ui-storybook.vercel.app). The competition model, the TEE backend and the Solana programs aren't mine. I built the front end against fixtures and stubs.

The front end ended up with two design systems: a warm orange one from January, and a dense chrome one for the trading screens that the build checks before a PR lands. I kept the old one instead of deleting it.

## Zeus, in ten days

The kit's first release was July 14, 2025. Then the repo sat quiet for five and a half months. Between January 3 and January 12, 2026 there are 120 commits, 68 of them on January 6: the landing page, SEO, trading components, a swap widget with tests, and a component registry.

The first design system shipped in that sprint. Internally it was Zeus: Sedona orange, Souvenir paired with Monument Grotesk, paper-texture shader backgrounds, a WebGL terminal effect, and over 100 tokens in light and dark.

I shipped the kit as a shadcn-compatible registry, so components install one at a time from the CLI instead of as one package: price chart, swap widget, token avatar, data table, elimination progress and a few more. That's more surface to maintain, and the registry got its own test config.

It wasn't clean. An automated review on January 7 turned up security issues and bugs, and a fix pass followed. The hero's browser mockup animation broke if you went straight to `/landing`, and a paper shader had a loading bug.

