## AeroDash

On April 26, 2026 I added AeroDash, a "y2k chrome" component family with 11 primitives and a playground at `/aerodash`. On May 4 it became the active Sedona design system.

It goes the other way from Zeus. Its doc calls it "a dense y2k chrome system for product UI, not a marketing kit": hard ink borders, cyan focus rings, offset underlays, clipped chrome tags, tabular numerals, compact uppercase labels. It's for the trading, competition and dashboard screens. Two of its rules: "Do not put page sections inside decorative cards," and a control never resizes when its text, hover, loading or selected state changes.

I didn't delete Zeus. It stays for older surfaces and registry work, and new screens use AeroDash unless a PR is explicitly scoped to the legacy kit. The cost is two systems in one repo.

AeroDash is also checked by the build. `manifest.ts` is the inventory, `index.ts` is the public API, and `scripts/audit-aerodash.mjs` uses the TypeScript compiler API to check consistency. It runs with the tests and the type check before a PR lands.

{% Figure src="/images/projects/sedona/aerodash-buttons.webp" alt="AeroDash component board 02: button anatomy with a left cap, label and right action well, a grid of button states, split buttons, segmented controls, chips and full-width CTA bars" caption="AeroDash board 02. Every button state has the same footprint, so nothing shifts when a control is hovered, pressed or disabled." width="wide" frame="browser" /%}

## The risograph plugin

I wanted charts that look printed, so I had a risograph halftone effect built as a Chart.js plugin. The first version was broken in several places: animation stalled and jumped, the riso line didn't animate at all, and half-wired grid and text drawing broke Chart.js rendering.

The March 17 rewrite plan split it into two lists. Keep the AM halftone tone lookup, the cached paper texture, multiply compositing and 20 performance optimizations. Rebuild everything else from scratch, with one global `printHeadX` driving all of the animation.

{% Gallery images=[{src: "/images/projects/sedona/riso-early.webp", alt: "Risograph line chart test capture: blue halftone fill under a very faint line, with the config panel on the right"}, {src: "/images/projects/sedona/riso-v2-working.webp", alt: "The v2-working capture of the same chart: halftone fill under a solid printed blue line, config panel on the right"}] columns="2" caption="Test captures from the repo, dummy data. Left: an earlier run where the line barely prints. Right: the v2-working capture after the rewrite." /%}

## The pitch deck

In August 2026 I redesigned Sedona's pitch deck, with agents doing the production. The deck is investor material, so none of its content is here, only the direction.

My brief asked for dither-kit charts, "very tuftian," a serious tone, and experiments on the style and a few key slides "before going deep." I prototyped in Paper. Then the notes, in order:

- I vetoed the first typeface pairing outright.
- "less dither I liked your previous version this is too much"
- "Mixing Orange and Blue on single slide is no no"
- "Let's do just blue in this -- keep Tuft maxxing every chart"
- "More tuftian chart this is slop"
- "Yes you cooked on the illus! Lets start on the remaining slides"

Two things reversed along the way. The dither got dialed back once I saw too much of it, and orange came off the chart slides. What's left is a small chart studio: ordered-dither charts exported at 3x, ink #021B33, blue #006DFF and cyan #00C8FF on paper white, and the wordmark and star as SVG.

## Where it stands

The docs lag the code. The CHANGELOG stops at 1.0.0 from January, and the README lists Zeus first while pointing to AeroDash as the source of truth. The repo moves in surges: July 2025, January 2026, then a handful of commits in April and May.
